# Port the local Node server listens on.
PORT=3002

# Sibling surfaces, for CORS and for resolving the session cookie ("browse the
# API while signed in"): the API verifies the console's signed cookie in-process,
# falling back to the console's /api/auth/get-session (APP_URL) on a cache miss.
# Production: https://app.flagon.io / https://www.flagon.io
APP_URL=http://localhost:3001
WEB_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Local Postgres (compose.yml at the repo root). One role, so it serves as both
# the app and owner connection locally; APP_DATABASE_URL / DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED
# below are only needed in production.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://flagon:flagon@127.0.0.1:5432/flagon

# --- Production --------------------------------------------------------------
# Managed Postgres gives you the OWNER role's two connection strings:
#   DATABASE_URL           (owner, pooled)
#   DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED  (owner, direct)  <- migrations use this automatically
# Keep those names as-is; they're the standard pooled/unpooled pair.
#
# The ONE secret you add by hand is the RLS-restricted app role (managed
# Postgres won't create it): its POOLED connection string, as APP_DATABASE_URL.
# The API prefers this over DATABASE_URL so runtime queries run as the
# restricted role and RLS is enforced. Convention for the role name: flagon_app.
# APP_DATABASE_URL=postgres://flagon_app:...@<pooled-host>/flagon?sslmode=require
#
# Optional override for the migration (owner) connection; defaults to
# DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED, then DATABASE_URL.
# MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL=

# --- Error tracking (Sentry) -------------------------------------------------
# OFF until a DSN is set: unset, the API reports nothing and behaves exactly as
# before. Set the DSN to turn it on, no code change.
# SENTRY_DSN=
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
# SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=0   # 0 = errors only (no performance tracing)

# --- Billing (Stripe) --------------------------------------------------------
# OFF until STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is set. The API owns billing (checkout, portal,
# webhook, subscription sync). The Pro price is resolved at runtime from the
# lookup_key "flagon_pro_monthly" — create a $20/mo recurring price with that
# lookup key in Stripe; there is NO price id to configure here.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...      (use a test key locally, live key in prod)
# The webhook signing secret. Locally, the compose `stripe` service prints it
# on startup (docker compose logs -f stripe) — stable per account, copy once.
# In prod, register https://api.flagon.io/webhooks/stripe and use its secret.
# Subscribe it to: checkout.session.completed, customer.subscription.created,
# customer.subscription.updated, customer.subscription.deleted, invoice.paid,
# invoice.payment_succeeded (the last two grant the $20 metered usage credit).
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
#
# On Vercel the webhook ALSO needs NODEJS_HELPERS=0 set on the project, or the
# runtime parses the body before we see it and every signature check fails. See
# src/lib/vercel-request.ts.
#
# Metered billing: run `node --env-file=.env --import tsx scripts/setup-metered.ts`
# once per Stripe account to create the `flagon_events` meter + metered price. The
# reporting cron (below) then reports usage to it; the credit grant offsets the $20.

# --- Cron (metered usage reporting sweep) ------------------------------------
# Bearer secret for /internal/cron/report. Vercel Cron injects it as
# `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>`. The endpoint fails CLOSED without it, so
# reporting only runs once this is set (and the vercel.json cron is active). Unset
# locally unless you want to exercise the sweep by hand.
# CRON_SECRET=a-long-random-string

# --- Auth (BetterAuth is hosted by the CONSOLE; the API only verifies) --------
# REQUIRED. The console SIGNS session cookies with this secret; the API uses it to
# VERIFY them in-process (getCookieCache). It MUST equal the console's value
# byte-for-byte, or the API rejects every session cookie. Match apps/app/.env.local.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=dev-secret-local-only-change-me-0123456789abcdef
# Unused by the API now (the console hosts BetterAuth). Kept optional for parity.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3002
# The console's concern now (it issues cookies); unused by the API.
# AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.flagon.io
# Auth email (verification / reset / invites) sends from the CONSOLE now. These
# stay only if the API sends its own mail; unset = console logger locally.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# EMAIL_FROM=Flagon <robin@flagon.io>

# --- Integration secrets (bring-your-own providers) --------------------------
# Bring-your-own integration credentials (e.g. a customer's Twilio auth token)
# are encrypted at rest with AES-256-GCM keyed off this value. OFF until set:
# configuring a BYO integration's credentials returns 503, everything else works.
# Any long, high-entropy string works (the AES key is derived via scrypt).
# Generate one with: openssl rand -base64 48
# Rotating it makes stored credentials undecryptable (customers re-enter them).
# INTEGRATIONS_SECRET_KEY=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# --- Object storage (Cloudflare R2 / any S3-compatible store) ----------------
# Powers uploads (org logos, project screenshots). OFF until configured: the
# upload endpoints return 503 and everything else behaves as before. All-or-
# nothing — set the whole core set or none. The values below point at the local
# ministack service (compose.yml), so uploads work out of the box once
# `npm run compose:up` is running. It's a LocalStack-style S3 emulator on :4566
# with dummy creds and path-style addressing.
STORAGE_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4566
STORAGE_REGION=us-east-1
STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=test
STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=test
STORAGE_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
STORAGE_PUBLIC_BUCKET=flagon-public
# Where the public bucket's objects are served from. Locally that's ministack's
# path-style object URL; in prod it's the R2 bucket's custom domain.
STORAGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4566/flagon-public
#
# --- Production (Cloudflare R2) ----------------------------------------------
# Same vars, pointed at R2. Create an R2 bucket, an R2 API token (S3 creds), and
# bind a custom domain (cdn.flagon.io) to the bucket for public reads. Region is
# "auto"; R2 accepts path-style, so STORAGE_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true is fine.
# STORAGE_ENDPOINT=https://<account_id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# STORAGE_REGION=auto
# STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<r2_access_key_id>
# STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<r2_secret_access_key>
# STORAGE_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
# STORAGE_PUBLIC_BUCKET=flagon-public
# STORAGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://cdn.flagon.io
